---

<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    author={author}
    authorUrl={authorUrl}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    category="SaaS"
    categorySlug="saas"
    headings={headings}
>
  <!-- Introduction -->
  <h2 id="introduction">Turn Your Knowledge Into Income with Online Courses</h2>
  <p>
    Spent eight months creating and selling courses on different platforms. Lost way too much money to transaction fees
    before figuring this out.
  </p>
  <p>
    Creating online courses? One of the best ways to build passive income as a remote worker. Create the content once,
    sell it forever. No trading hours for dollars.
  </p>
  <p>
    But picking the wrong platform costs you. Some slice off huge chunks of your revenue. Others hit you with surprise
    fees. Some are so complicated you'll waste weeks just figuring out how to upload a video.
  </p>
  <p>
    Tested ten course platforms over three months. Created actual courses. Processed real sales. Tracked what worked and
    what sucked.
  </p>
  <p>
    Quick answer if you're in a hurry: <a href="#teachable">Teachable</a> for most people. Easiest to use, solid free
    tier, doesn't annoy you daily. <a href="#kajabi">Kajabi</a> if you want email, funnels, and website all in one
    place. <a href="#thinkific">Thinkific</a> if you want zero transaction fees and maximum control.
  </p>

  <!-- Quick Picks -->
  <h2 id="quick-picks">Our Top 3 Course Platform Picks</h2>
  <p>Short on time? Here's what to use:</p>
  <ComparisonTable
      columns={quickPicksColumns}
      rows={quickPicksRows}
      caption="Top 3 online course platforms for creators"
  />
  <p>
    All three let you test free or offer trial periods. Read on for detailed breakdowns of all eight platforms worth
    considering.
  </p>

  <!-- Why Sell Courses Online -->
  <h2 id="why-sell-courses">Why Courses Work for Remote Workers</h2>
  <p>
    Online courses are ridiculously scalable. Here's why creators are all over this:
  </p>

  <h3>Create Once, Sell Forever</h3>
  <p>
    Unlike consulting where you trade time for money, courses scale infinitely. Your hundredth student costs you nothing
    extra to serve but adds pure profit.
  </p>
  <p>
    Top course creators earn six and seven figures from courses they created years ago. Even modest success—a two
    hundred dollar course selling ten copies monthly—generates twenty-four grand annually in mostly passive income.
  </p>

  <h3>Build Authority</h3>
  <p>
    Publishing a course makes you the expert in your field. Opens doors to speaking gigs, consulting, higher-paying
    clients.
  </p>
  <p>
    Courses build trust with your audience in ways free content can't. Someone who drops two hundred bucks on your
    course pays way more attention than someone skimming a free blog post.
  </p>

  <h3>Help More People</h3>
  <p>
    Ever wished you could help more people? Courses let you do exactly that. Your knowledge reaches students across time
    zones while you sleep. No longer limited by your available hours.
  </p>

  <h3>The Market Is Huge</h3>
  <p>
    Global e-learning market exceeds three hundred billion and grows ten to fifteen percent annually. Remote work
    accelerated demand for online skills training. People are actively searching for courses in nearly every niche.
  </p>

  <!-- Detailed Reviews -->
  <h2 id="detailed-reviews">Course Platform Detailed Reviews</h2>

  <!-- Teachable -->
  <h3 id="teachable">Teachable — Easiest for First-Time Creators</h3>
  <p>
    This one pioneered modern course platforms and remains the easiest way to get your first course online. The
    interface means you'll spend time creating content instead of wrestling with software.
  </p>
  <p>
    What's good: Course builder is genuinely beginner-friendly. Drag and drop lessons, upload videos, your course is
    ready to sell. Built-in payment processing means no merchant account headaches. Teachable handles everything.
  </p>
  <p>
    You get:<br/>
    Drag-and-drop course builder for creating content.<br/>
    Accept credit cards and PayPal instantly.<br/>
    Track student progress, completion rates, engagement.<br/>
    Sell one-on-one or group coaching alongside courses.<br/>
    Built-in page builder for marketing your courses.<br/>
    Let others promote your courses for commission.<br/>
    Free tier costs one dollar plus ten percent per sale.
  </p>
  <p>
    Pricing runs free plan with one dollar plus ten percent transaction fee. Basic at fifty-nine monthly with five
    percent fee. Pro at one fifty-nine monthly with zero percent fee. Pro Plus at two forty-nine monthly. Annual billing
    saves thirty-three percent.
  </p>
  <p>
    What I experienced: Had a course live within two hours of signing up. Learning curve is minimal. Built-in payment
    processing removed a major friction point.
  </p>
  <p>
    Makes sense for: First-time course creators, solo entrepreneurs, anyone who wants the simplest path from idea to
    selling. Great for validating course ideas quickly.
  </p>
  <p>
    What's not great: Transaction fees on lower tiers add up quickly at scale. Customization options are limited
    compared to Thinkific. No built-in email marketing. You'll need ConvertKit or similar.
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/teachable"
      text="Start Free with Teachable — Easiest Course Platform"
      variant="primary"
      program="teachable"
  />

  <!-- Thinkific -->
  <h3 id="thinkific">Thinkific — Maximum Customization</h3>
  <p>
    Gives creators more control over their course experience and keeps more money in your pocket with zero transaction
    fees on all plans. Want flexibility without paying Kajabi prices? Thinkific delivers.
  </p>
  <p>
    What's good: No transaction fees means you keep one hundred percent of your sales minus payment processor fees.
    Course builder offers more customization than Teachable including better quiz and assignment features.
  </p>
  <p>
    You get:<br/>
    Keep all your revenue on every plan with zero transaction fees.<br/>
    Quizzes assignments certificates and prerequisites.<br/>
    Create a complete course website not just course pages.<br/>
    Built-in community spaces for students.<br/>
    Extend functionality with third-party integrations.<br/>
    Remove Thinkific branding on higher plans.<br/>
    Free tier includes one course unlimited students zero percent transaction fee.
  </p>
  <p>
    Pricing runs free plan for one course. Basic at forty-nine monthly. Start at ninety-nine monthly. Grow at one
    ninety-nine monthly. Annual billing offers significant savings.
  </p>
  <p>
    What I experienced: Thinkific's free plan is surprisingly generous. One full course with unlimited students.
    Learning curve is slightly steeper than Teachable but worth it for the flexibility.
  </p>
  <p>
    Makes sense for: Creators who want zero transaction fees, more customization options, or stronger quiz and
    assessment features. Good for course-based businesses at scale.
  </p>
  <p>
    What's not great: Interface is less intuitive than Teachable initially. No native email marketing requires
    integration. Advanced features require higher-tier plans.
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/thinkific"
      text="Try Thinkific Free — Zero Transaction Fees"
      variant="primary"
      program="thinkific"
  />

  <!-- Kajabi -->
  <h3 id="kajabi">Kajabi — Everything in One Place</h3>
  <p>
    This is the premium choice for creators who want everything in one place. Courses email marketing sales funnels
    website and community. Price is steep but it replaces multiple tools.
  </p>
  <p>
    What's good: You genuinely don't need any other tools. Email sequences landing pages checkout affiliate program
    podcast hosting. All built in. For creators tired of stitching together multiple services Kajabi simplifies
    everything.
  </p>
  <p>
    You get:<br/>
    Courses email website funnels in one tool.<br/>
    Built-in email with automation and sequences.<br/>
    Pre-built pipeline templates for conversions.<br/>
    Native community features for student engagement.<br/>
    Host and distribute podcasts directly.<br/>
    Keep all your revenue with zero transaction fees.<br/>
    Branded mobile app for your students.
  </p>
  <p>
    Pricing runs Basic at one forty-nine monthly for three products and ten thousand contacts. Growth at one ninety-nine
    monthly for fifteen products and twenty-five thousand contacts. Pro at three ninety-nine monthly for one hundred
    products and one hundred thousand contacts. Fourteen-day free trial.
  </p>
  <p>
    What I experienced: Kajabi's value becomes clear when you calculate what you'd spend on separate tools for email
    funnels and course hosting. For serious course businesses it often saves money.
  </p>
  <p>
    Makes sense for: Established creators building a full business coaches with multiple offers anyone who values
    simplicity over cost optimization.
  </p>
  <p>
    What's not great: Expensive for beginners. One forty-nine monthly is steep before you've validated your course. Some
    features are less powerful than specialized tools. Limited customization compared to Thinkific.
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/kajabi"
      text="Try Kajabi Free for 14 Days — All-in-One Platform"
      variant="primary"
      program="kajabi"
  />

  <!-- Podia -->
  <h3 id="podia">Podia — Sell Anything Digital</h3>
  <p>
    Positions itself as the platform for selling anything digital. Courses downloads memberships coaching and webinars.
    If you sell multiple product types Podia keeps everything organized in one place.
  </p>
  <p>
    What's good: The simplicity is refreshing. No transaction fees unlimited everything and a clean interface. Podia
    also includes email marketing eliminating one more tool from your stack.
  </p>
  <p>
    You get:<br/>
    Courses downloads memberships coaching webinars.<br/>
    Keep one hundred percent of your sales.<br/>
    Email marketing and automation included.<br/>
    No caps on products customers or emails.<br/>
    Built-in affiliate program.<br/>
    Create your entire site in Podia.<br/>
    Built-in messaging with customers.
  </p>
  <p>
    Pricing runs Mover at thirty-nine monthly with email included. Shaker at eighty-nine monthly with affiliates and
    advanced features. Fourteen-day free trial. No free tier.
  </p>
  <p>
    What I experienced: Podia is wonderfully simple. If you sell a mix of courses downloads and memberships having
    everything in one dashboard is genuinely valuable.
  </p>
  <p>
    Makes sense for: Creators selling multiple digital product types those who want built-in email marketing anyone who
    values simplicity over advanced features.
  </p>
  <p>
    What's not great: Course features are less robust than Teachable or Thinkific. No free tier means upfront
    commitment. Limited quiz and assessment options.
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/podia"
      text="Try Podia Free for 14 Days — Sell Anything Digital"
      variant="secondary"
      program="podia"
  />

  <!-- Skillshare -->
  <h3 id="skillshare">Skillshare — Creative Skills Marketplace</h3>
  <p>
    Marketplace platform where students pay a subscription to access all courses. You earn based on minutes watched
    rather than course sales. Fundamentally different model.
  </p>
  <p>
    What's good: Built-in audience of millions of students means you don't need your own marketing. Skillshare promotes
    your courses to interested learners. Great for creative skills where the platform audience aligns well.
  </p>
  <p>
    You get:<br/>
    Millions of active subscribers.<br/>
    Skillshare promotes courses internally so no marketing required.<br/>
    Earn based on premium minutes watched.<br/>
    No upfront costs to create courses.<br/>
    Strong in design illustration photography.<br/>
    Students complete projects for engagement.
  </p>
  <p>
    Pricing runs free to publish. Teachers earn royalties from a pool based on minutes watched. Typical earnings range
    from five cents to ten cents per minute watched.
  </p>
  <p>
    What I experienced: Skillshare works well for building initial audience and exposure but earnings per student are
    lower than selling your own courses. Best as a supplement not primary platform.
  </p>
  <p>
    Makes sense for: Creative skills teachers those without existing audiences creators who want passive discovery
    without marketing.
  </p>
  <p>
    What's not great: Lower earnings per student than direct sales. You don't own the customer relationship. Course
    length affects earnings significantly.
  </p>
  <p class="text-white/80 italic">
    Note: Skillshare doesn't offer an affiliate program. Including it because it's a legitimate option for creators.
  </p>

  <!-- Udemy -->
  <h3 id="udemy">Udemy — Massive Built-In Marketplace</h3>
  <p>
    Largest course marketplace with fifty-seven million students. Publishing on Udemy gives you instant access to
    massive discovery potential but at the cost of significant revenue share.
  </p>
  <p>
    What's good: The marketplace effect is real. Students searching for topics discover your course without you doing
    any marketing. For new creators without audiences this discovery is valuable.
  </p>
  <p>
    You get:<br/>
    Fifty-seven million students in massive built-in marketplace.<br/>
    Courses rank in Google and Udemy search.<br/>
    No platform fees upfront free to publish.<br/>
    Reviews and ratings for social proof drive more sales.<br/>
    Students access courses on iOS and Android.<br/>
    Built-in student support tools.
  </p>
  <p>
    Pricing runs free to publish. Revenue share is thirty-seven percent for Udemy-generated sales ninety-seven percent
    for instructor-generated sales with coupon. Frequent sales pressure pricing down.
  </p>
  <p>
    What I experienced: Udemy is excellent for discovery but frustrating for pricing. Constant sales mean your two
    hundred dollar course often sells for twelve ninety-nine. Best combined with your own platform.
  </p>
  <p>
    Makes sense for: New creators without audiences supplemental income alongside your own platform topics with high
    search volume.
  </p>
  <p>
    What's not great: Heavy revenue share up to sixty-three percent. Constant discounting devalues courses. You don't
    own customer relationships. Pricing pressure is significant.
  </p>
  <p class="text-white/80 italic">
    Note: Including Udemy as a legitimate option though we don't earn commissions from recommending it.
  </p>

  <!-- Gumroad -->
  <h3 id="gumroad">Gumroad — Ultra Simple</h3>
  <p>
    Simplest way to sell anything digital. Courses ebooks templates software. Want minimal friction between your content
    and customers' credit cards? Gumroad delivers.
  </p>
  <p>
    What's good: You can literally have something for sale in ten minutes. No complex setup no learning curve. Just
    upload set a price and share your link. The ten percent fee is simple to understand.
  </p>
  <p>
    You get:<br/>
    Sell anything in minutes with extreme simplicity.<br/>
    Ten percent of sales no monthly fees flat fee structure.<br/>
    Basic email features included.<br/>
    Sell subscriptions and memberships.<br/>
    Gumroad discover page for exposure and audience building.<br/>
    Fixed price pay-what-you-want or free flexible pricing.<br/>
    No caps on what you sell unlimited products.
  </p>
  <p>
    Pricing runs free to use. Gumroad takes ten percent of each sale plus payment processing approximately two point
    nine percent plus thirty cents.
  </p>
  <p>
    What I experienced: Gumroad is perfect for quick launches and testing ideas. The ten percent fee is higher than
    dedicated platforms at scale but the simplicity is worth it for smaller creators.
  </p>
  <p>
    Makes sense for: Quick course launches testing course ideas creators who sell multiple digital product types anyone
    who values simplicity above all else.
  </p>
  <p>
    What's not great: Course features are basic. No quizzes certificates or advanced student tracking. Ten percent adds
    up at scale. Limited customization options.
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/gumroad"
