  <!-- How to Choose -->
  <h2 id="how-to-choose">How to Choose</h2>
  <p>
    With so many solid options here's how to pick the right platform for your specific situation:
  </p>

  <h3>By Experience Level</h3>
  <p>
    Complete beginner? Teachable for easiest setup.<br/>
    Some tech comfort? Thinkific for more features.<br/>
    Established creator? Kajabi for all-in-one.<br/>
    Just testing an idea? Gumroad for fastest launch.
  </p>

  <h3>By Business Model</h3>
  <p>
    Single course focus? Teachable or Thinkific.<br/>
    Multiple products? Podia or Kajabi.<br/>
    Coaching plus courses? Kajabi or Teachable.<br/>
    Corporate training? LearnWorlds or Thinkific Plus.
  </p>

  <h3>By Budget</h3>
  <p>
    Free to start? Gumroad Thinkific Free or Teachable Free.<br/>
    Under fifty monthly? Podia at thirty-nine or Thinkific Basic at forty-nine.<br/>
    Under one hundred monthly? Teachable Pro or Thinkific Start.<br/>
    Premium all-in-one? Kajabi at one forty-nine and up.
  </p>

  <h3>My Recommendation</h3>
  <p>
    Start with free tiers. Test Teachable or Thinkific free to validate your course idea.<br/>
    Upgrade when needed. Move to paid plans when transaction fees exceed subscription cost.<br/>
    Consider all-in-one later. Kajabi makes sense once you're earning consistently.<br/>
    Don't overthink it. The platform matters less than creating and marketing your course.
  </p>

  <!-- Getting Started -->
  <h2 id="getting-started">Getting Started</h2>
  <p>
    Ready to create your course? Here's a practical roadmap:
  </p>

  <h3>Validate Your Idea</h3>
  <p>
    Before building a full course confirm people will pay for it. Pre-sell to your audience create a mini-course or
    survey potential students. Validation prevents wasted effort on courses nobody wants.
  </p>

  <h3>Outline Your Curriculum</h3>
  <p>
    Map out the transformation students will experience. Start with the end result then work backward to identify the
    steps needed to get there. Break those steps into modules and lessons.
  </p>

  <h3>Create Your Content</h3>
  <p>
    Start recording. Don't aim for perfection. Aim for done. Your first course won't be your best and that's fine.
    Students care about results not production quality.
  </p>

  <h3>Set Up Your Platform</h3>
  <p>
    Upload your content to your chosen platform create your sales page and configure checkout. Most platforms make this
    straightforward. You can be live within a day.
  </p>

  <h3>Launch and Iterate</h3>
  <p>
    Launch to your audience gather feedback and improve. Your course gets better with each cohort of students. Focus on
    helping students succeed and the rest follows.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ Section -->
  <FAQSection faqs={faqs}/>

  <!-- Conclusion -->
  <h2 id="conclusion">Final Picks</h2>
  <p>
    After three months of testing across multiple platforms here are my final recommendations:
  </p>
  <p>
    For most creators Teachable is my top pick. The combination of ease of use reasonable pricing and solid features
    makes it the best starting point. You'll be selling courses within hours not days.
  </p>
  <p>
    For maximum control Thinkific offers zero transaction fees and better customization. Want to own more of your course
    experience and keep one hundred percent of revenue? Thinkific is excellent.
  </p>
  <p>
    For serious course businesses Kajabi is worth the premium price if you want email marketing funnels and website in
    one platform. The math works once you're earning consistently.
  </p>
  <p>
    For quick validation Gumroad gets you selling in minutes with no monthly fees. Perfect for testing course ideas
    before committing to a dedicated platform.
  </p>
  <p>
    The best platform is the one you'll actually use. Start with a free tier create your first course and upgrade when
    your business justifies the investment. Your audience is waiting for what you have to teach.
  </p>

